  "metadata": {
    "title": "Oracle's Record Quarter Isn't Enough to Justify the Capital Ask",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "This article first appeared on GuruFocus.\nOracle (NYSE:ORCL) fell 9.35% in premarket despite reporting record Q4 revenue of $19.2 billion, up 21% year-over-year, with cloud infrastructure alone growing 93% to $5.8 billion. Q4 GAAP EPS rose 21% to $1.45 and non-GAAP EPS climbed 24% to $2.11. The non-GAAP figure was boosted by a one-time investment gain. Stripping that out, non-GAAP EPS was $2.03. Remaining performance obligations jumped $85 billion sequentially to $638 billion, up 363% year-over-year, but $75 billion of that total reflects prepaid or customer-supplied GPU hardware rather than traditional contracted revenue.\nFor the full year, Oracle posted revenue of $67.4 billion, up 17%, with IaaS up 77% to $18.1 billion and operating cash flow hitting a record $32 billion, up 54%. Free cash flow was negative $23.7 billion as capital expenditures reached $55.7 billion, more than double the prior year. CEO Clay Magouyrk said Q1 FY2027 delivery is approaching 1 gigawatt of computing capacity, nearly equal to all of FY2026 combined.\nFor Q1 FY2027, Oracle guided total revenue growth of 27%-29% and cloud revenue growth of 58%-64%, with non-GAAP EPS of $1.72-$1.76. The company confirmed its full-year FY2027 revenue target of $90 billion and raised non-GAAP EPS guidance to $8.05. For FY2027, Oracle expects to raise approximately $40 billion through a combination of debt and equity financing, including a previously announced $20 billion at-the-market equity issuance, after raising $43 billion in debt and $5 billion in equity in FY2026.\nThe market's reaction appears focused on the plan to raise another $40 billion in FY2027 and a $90 billion revenue target that was confirmed rather than raised.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "This article first appeared on GuruFocus.",
      "Oracle (NYSE:ORCL) fell 9.35% in premarket despite reporting record Q4 revenue of $19.2 billion, up 21% year-over-year, with cloud infrastructure alone growing 93% to $5.8 billion. Q4 GAAP EPS rose 21% to $1.45 and non-GAAP EPS climbed 24% to $2.11. The non-GAAP figure was boosted by a one-time investment gain. Stripping that out, non-GAAP EPS was $2.03. Remaining performance obligations jumped $85 billion sequentially to $638 billion, up 363% year-over-year, but $75 billion of that total reflects prepaid or customer-supplied GPU hardware rather than traditional contracted revenue.",
      "For the full year, Oracle posted revenue of $67.4 billion, up 17%, with IaaS up 77% to $18.1 billion and operating cash flow hitting a record $32 billion, up 54%. Free cash flow was negative $23.7 billion as capital expenditures reached $55.7 billion, more than double the prior year. CEO Clay Magouyrk said Q1 FY2027 delivery is approaching 1 gigawatt of computing capacity, nearly equal to all of FY2026 combined.",
      "For Q1 FY2027, Oracle guided total revenue growth of 27%-29% and cloud revenue growth of 58%-64%, with non-GAAP EPS of $1.72-$1.76. The company confirmed its full-year FY2027 revenue target of $90 billion and raised non-GAAP EPS guidance to $8.05. For FY2027, Oracle expects to raise approximately $40 billion through a combination of debt and equity financing, including a previously announced $20 billion at-the-market equity issuance, after raising $43 billion in debt and $5 billion in equity in FY2026.",
      "The market's reaction appears focused on the plan to raise another $40 billion in FY2027 and a $90 billion revenue target that was confirmed rather than raised."
    ],
